  "full_response": "Below is a sample README file that demonstrates how to use `curl` commands for API requests with various authentication methods, including Bearer tokens, API keys, and basic authentication credentials.\n\n---\n\n# API Interaction using cURL\n\nThis README provides examples of `curl` commands used to interact with our API. It demonstrates how to include authentication headers for Bearer tokens, API keys, and basic authentication credentials.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- Ensure you have `curl` installed on your system. You can download it from [https://curl.se/](https://curl.se/) or use a package manager like `apt`, `brew`, or `choco`.\n- Access to the API endpoint.\n- Appropriate authentication credentials: Bearer Token, API Key, and Basic Auth credentials.\n\n## Authentication Methods\n\n### 1. Bearer Token\n\nBearer tokens are widely used for OAuth-based authentication. Below is an example of how to include a Bearer token in your request.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \\\n  https://api.example.com/v1/resource \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN` with your actual bearer token and adjust the URL to match the appropriate API endpoint.\n\n### 2. API Key\n\nTo use an API key, you typically include it in the headers of your HTTP request. Here’s an example:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \\\n  https://api.example.com/v1/resource \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: YOUR_API_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your actual API key.\n\n### 3. Basic Authentication\n\nBasic authentication involves encoding your username and password in base64 and including them in the headers of your request.\n\nFirst, encode your credentials:\n\n```bash\necho -n \"username:password\" | base64\n```\n\nUse the encoded result in your cURL command:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \\\n  https://api.example.com/v1/resource \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Basic YOUR_BASE64_ENCODED_CREDENTIALS\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_BASE64_ENCODED_CREDENTIALS` with the output from the above encoding command.\n\n## Example Usage\n\nAfter setting up your authentication method of choice, you can start making requests to your desired API endpoints. Here's a sample GET request to fetch a list of resources:\n\n",
